WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:06.080
My surname's fab!

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:10.000
Yeah, well it's a big deal isn't it?

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:20.120
I think we always imagine that

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:23.280
all the generations
of women before us

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:26.360
were perfectly happy
to lose their maiden name

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:27.840
and take their husband's name.

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:29.760
And I'm sure that's not true.

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:34.520
So, in Britain, patrilineal surnames
go down through the male line,

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:36.360
down through the generations.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:41.080
Surname systems aren't the same
all the way around the world.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.520
Yes, smash the patriarchy.

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:58.960
I had read about a couple where
the husband took the wife's name

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:00.440
and I thought,
"Oh that's interesting."

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:03.400
You said, "It's our choice

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:07.800
and if it means that much to you
then I'll take your name."

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:09.400
And I cried.

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:14.640
But my dad was really confused,
and it's his name.

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:18.360
He was just like, "Is that legal?"
Like, "Yes".

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:26.880
I was Brian Macken.

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:28.680
And I was Rhona Wells.

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:31.280
And then afterwards
we were both Mackenwells,

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:33.200
one word without a dash in between.

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:38.280
And I really wanted us to be
all one name, sort of family unit.

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:41.040
I believe there's a rabbit farm
somewhere in the north of England

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:42.880
that is called Mackenwells.

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:44.600
There's only three of us
in the world.

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:46.640
Yeah, exactly, and a rabbit farm.

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:47.800
And a rabbit farm!

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:54.440
So my dad is called Clive,
my mum is called Anne.

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:57.000
Clive and Anne... Cliv... Anne

00:01:57.600 --> 00:01:59.280
Clyfan - that's my surname.

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:01.760
Anncliv is a bit gross,
isn't it? Annecliv.

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:04.920
Whereas "Clyfan!"
That just rolls off the tongue.

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:09.280
My parents, they didn't
just want me to kind of

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:11.400
lazily take the surname of my dad.

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:12.720
The second thing is that my mum

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:14.960
really wanted people
to know that I was Welsh.

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:17.640
My first girlfriend Flora,

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:19.960
we thought if we had a kid
that it could be called Flobin.

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:26.880
In Spain, people have
double-barrelled surnames.

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:31.480
The father's surname is also
followed by the mother's surname.

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:34.720
That actually reveals the ancestry
through the male line

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:37.600
but from the mother's side
and from the father's side as well.

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:41.720
Pablo Picasso, his mum's last name
is actually Picasso

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:43.560
and he just used Picasso.

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:45.840
My mum is doing the family tree,

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:50.160
and she has managed to get to
the 15th Century, which is a lot.

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:54.720
Let's look at gender equality
in surnames.

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:57.040
So if everyone
has a double-barrelled name

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:00.520
and you only pass along the surname
according to your gender,

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:02.760
that actually solves
a lot of problems.

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:06.840
Let's say we have, in the far future,

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:11.000
Vivienne Jolie-Pitt married
to Levon Thurman-Hawke.

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:13.080
Vivienne, because she's a woman,

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:15.520
would take her mum's name, Jolie.

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:20.480
And Levon, because he's a man,
would take his dad's name, Hawke.

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:23.560
So you get, the Jolie-Hawkes.

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:26.080
So this system has loads
of advantages.

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:29.120
It's really fair to both sides
of the family,

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:41.320
It works just as well
for same sex couples

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:43.520
as it does for
opposite sex couples

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:45.240
because really you can just choose

00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:48.600
whichever of the surnames
to keep and pass on.

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:52.680
It makes exploring
your ancestry way easier.

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:58.160
In 1934,

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:04.080
the law was passed that every Turkish
citizen had to adopt a surname.

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:08.800
There was a rush on all these
fancy names like Yildirim,

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:10.920
which means flash or lightning.

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:13.920
You have a lot of these names
in Turkish like hawk,

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:15.400
wolf,

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:16.440
lion,

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:17.720
brave,

00:04:17.800 --> 00:04:18.880
or tall.

00:04:19.240 --> 00:04:21.880
So Ozturk meaning "the real Turk",

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:23.760
I was born and raised in Germany,

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:26.880
and my children, they're going
to be here living in the UK.

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:32.760
I think maybe my grandfather

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:35.760
would have been really
disappointed by that, you know.

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:43.840
One surname is used
for professional communication.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:46.160
The other one is for personal.

00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:50.520
The online persona might
actually be more significant

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:52.360
than our legal documents.

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:56.960
Because it provides much more
information about who we are.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:06.240
People seem to accept a surname
not as a matter of choice.

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:08.040
That may be changing.

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:11.800
If so, that's probably a good thing.

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:13.600
So, it has made me who I am

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:15.080
but that's part of
a much broader picture

00:05:15.160 --> 00:05:17.520
about where the name comes from
and the reasons for doing it.

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:20.320
Just the fact that we
have a choice, I think,

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:23.040
it doesn’t matter what you
choose but choose actively.

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:25.480
Yeah there's nothing wrong with
taking your husband's name.

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:26.440
If you want to.

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:28.840
If you had a better last name
I might well have taken it.

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:30.240
If my name was Tolkien Shakespeare

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:34.080
then you'd have had a hard time
arguing not to take that name.

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:36.080
Maybe... maybe.

